      <h2>What This Means for eBook Creators</h2>

      <p>For self-publishers and authors:</p>

      <ol>
        <li><strong>Start using AVIF for embedded images</strong> in your EPUB files</li>
        <li><strong>Test on multiple devices</strong> before release—AVIF support is still inconsistent</li>
        <li><strong>Provide fallback PNG/JPEG</strong> via secondary sources for readers with older devices</li>
        <li><strong>Document format requirements</strong> in your book description or author page</li>
      </ol>

      <p>The industry is moving toward AVIF, but mainstream adoption will take years. For now, dual-format distribution (AVIF main + PNG fallback) is the safest approach for image-heavy books.</p>

      <h2>Bottom Line: Why This Update Matters Now</h2>

      <p>Kindle's AVIF support is not merely a minor format addition. It represents <strong>Amazon's recognition that visual content is becoming central to the eBook experience</strong>.</p>

      <p>As e-ink hardware improves—higher DPI, faster refresh rates, color displays—AVIF will become increasingly relevant. Readers who invest in AVIF-compatible devices today will benefit from smaller, higher-quality libraries tomorrow.</p>

      <p>For now, the best strategy is to <strong>future-proof your workflow</strong> by adopting AVIF tools and formats, while maintaining fallback options for legacy devices.</p>

